WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.720
Hodgkin and Huxley pushed a fine electrode inside a squid giant axon.

2
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.240
At the peak of the impulse, the inside turned positive.

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.380
The old theory said the voltage should stop near zero.

4
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.060
It overshot. Something let charge in faster than it could leak out.

5
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:25.820
What was it?

6
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:30.113
To find out, they held the membrane voltage fixed.

7
00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:32.193
This is a voltage clamp.

8
00:00:33.113 --> 00:00:39.133
In the squid axon, a step gave an early inward current, then a delayed outward

9
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:43.893
current. They replaced most of the outside sodium with choline.

10
00:00:44.573 --> 00:00:46.053
The early current reversed.

11
00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:48.593
The delayed current was little affected.

12
00:00:49.393 --> 00:00:53.353
So the early current is sodium, and the delayed current is potassium.

13
00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:57.087
Each current follows one rule.

14
00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:01.347
Current equals conductance times driving force.

15
00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:07.327
The driving force is the gap between the membrane voltage and the ion's equilibrium

16
00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:12.787
voltage. Take a model squid axon, with illustrative round numbers.

17
00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:17.147
Sodium's equilibrium voltage is +50 millivolts.

18
00:01:18.027 --> 00:01:21.767
Step the membrane to 0 millivolts with sodium conductance 20.

19
00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:28.447
The driving force is −50, so the current is −1000 microamperes per

20
00:01:28.447 --> 00:01:29.947
square centimetre, inward.

21
00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:35.347
For potassium, the equilibrium voltage is −77 millivolts.

22
00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:41.527
With conductance 15, the current is +1155, outward.

23
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.580
Now let the model run free.

24
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.740
In the Hodgkin-Huxley model of the squid axon, past threshold, sodium conductance

25
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.860
climbs and sodium flows in.

26
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.920
The voltage rises toward sodium's equilibrium voltage.

27
00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.560
That raises sodium conductance further.

28
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.800
This is the rising phase.

29
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.180
The peak falls short of +50 millivolts. Inactivation is already shutting sodium

30
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.140
off, and potassium is rising.

31
00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:14.303
Inactivation then shuts off the sodium current.

32
00:02:15.043 --> 00:02:17.343
Potassium current drives the voltage down.

33
00:02:18.123 --> 00:02:19.583
This is the falling phase.

34
00:02:20.503 --> 00:02:24.163
Potassium conductance falls slowly, so it stays raised.

35
00:02:24.883 --> 00:02:29.543
It pulls the voltage toward potassium's equilibrium voltage, below rest.

36
00:02:30.363 --> 00:02:31.643
This is the undershoot.

37
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:37.077
Right after a spike, most sodium channels are inactivated.

38
00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:43.937
No stimulus, however strong, triggers a second spike, because too few sodium

39
00:02:43.937 --> 00:02:45.077
channels are available.

40
00:02:46.497 --> 00:02:51.997
An impulse travels because its inward current spreads along the core of the axon.

41
00:02:52.537 --> 00:02:55.677
It brings the next stretch of membrane to threshold.

42
00:02:56.837 --> 00:03:02.537
The membrane just behind cannot be excited again, so the impulse travels one way.

43
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:05.857
Keep this rule for the squid axon.

44
00:03:06.777 --> 00:03:08.597
Sodium entering makes the spike.

45
00:03:09.517 --> 00:03:12.097
Inactivation and potassium end it.
